  "mechaber/part-001.txt": {
    "1:main": "The place and order of kneading matzot. And in it are 6 seifim: One should not knead in a place of sunlight, and on a cloudy day it is forbidden under the entire open sky, because on a cloudy day the sun is everywhere. {Rama: And therefore they are accustomed to be careful not to knead opposite an open window even where there is no sun, lest it become cloudy without his realizing it (Terumat HaDeshen siman 193). And similarly one should be careful not to carry the matzot to the oven under the open sky, and similarly the water or the flour (Hagahot Maimoni ch. 5 in the name of Semak and Mahariv).} And one should not approach to knead near the oven because of the heat of the oven.",
    "2:main": "One should not leave the dough without occupation even for a single moment; and all the while they are occupied with it, even the entire day, it does not leaven. If they left it without occupation for the measure of a mil, it becomes chametz. The measure of a mil is a quarter of an hour and one twentieth of an hour. {Rama: And one should be strict to hurry regarding making matzot, for there is concern that the delays will combine to the measure of a mil, or that it will be in a hot place where it leavens quickly (Hagahot Maimoni ch. 5 and Mordechai perek Elu Overin).} And after they occupied themselves with the dough and it warmed in their hands, if they leave it without occupation it leavens immediately. If they began with two doughs as one and one leavened, it is known that the second also leavened even if one does not see in it signs of leavening. If it leavened to the point that there are cracks in it — even if the cracks did not intermingle with one another but one goes here and one goes there — it is complete chametz, and one who eats it is liable for karet. If there are no cracks in it but only that it paled (meaning: its appearance changed toward white — Aruch) — its face like a person whose hair stands on end — one who eats it is exempt. {Rama: And if the matzah was formed and it appears it will not come to leavening, it is permitted to break it and return to form it so that the occupation will nullify the leavening; however, it is good to be careful l'chatchila (Maharil in the name of Agudah and Rokeach).}",
    "3:main": "If the woman who is kneading is shaping the dough with water, she should not shape it with that water with which she cooled her hands, because they become heated and leaven the dough; rather she should have another vessel full of water with which to shape it. {Rama: However, if she transgressed and did not cool her hands, it is permitted (Rambam ch. 5).}",
    "4:main": "If one kneaded after the time when chametz becomes forbidden, these waters together with the other waters with which they wash the kneading trough — one should not spill them except in a sloped place so that they not collect in one place and leaven. {Rama: And the vessels with which they form the matzot and the knife with which they cut the dough — one should scrape them constantly at the time of production so that dough not adhere to them; and after production one should rinse them and dry them well to return and use them a second time (Maharil). And this is preferable to what people are accustomed to scrape them, for it is impossible to clean them well by scraping alone; and one who is strict will scrape them and afterward rinse them (his own opinion). And on Yom Tov it is permitted to do this via an akum; and if there is no akum, one should put them in the cold or place them in the oven so the dough on them will bake. And kneading troughs into which one can place something — it is permitted to rinse them, and similarly all comparable cases. And one should be careful with a trough in which one kneads that there be no hole or crack from which one cannot pick out the dough after kneading, and it will leaven and afterward when one returns to knead in it it will mix with the dough (Maharil). And one should not place the trough on a pillow or wool cover at the time of kneading, for it heats the trough; but on leather it is permitted (Mahariv).}",
    "5:main": "If she transgressed and kneaded under the sun, the bread is permitted.",
    "6:main": "If the dough is soft, one should not add flour to it; rather one makes a small dough from a hard mixture and mixes it with the soft dough. {Rama: And one should distance the matzot from the flour, for flour that clings to them later goes into cooked food and leavens (Mordechai beginning of perek Elu Overin).}"
  },
